                    "fan_type": "variable_speed",
                    "minimum_flow_fraction": 0.3,
                    "minimum_flow_fraction_howto": "HOW THE FAN TURNDOWN IS SET: minimum_flow_fraction is the lowest airflow this unit runs at, as a fraction of design airflow (0.3, the median turndown of the ten-product Challenge lab testing). The measure uses it, or the minimum outdoor-air ratio when that is higher, for three things: the supply airflow when there is no heating or cooling load, the lowest airflow adjust_cfm_per_ton_per_limits may cut a lower stage to when keeping cfm/ton inside EnergyPlus's operating range, and the EnergyPlus Fan Power Minimum Flow Fraction, so fan power follows the airflow down to this value. A variable-speed fan modulates continuously, so the fan floor is this value and not the lowest compressor stage in stage_flow_fractions_heating / stage_flow_fractions_cooling; the compressor stages run at their own airflows, the lowest of them at this value (stage 1 set to 0.3 by decision 2026-10-28).",
                    "fan_efficiency_howto": "HOW FAN EFFICIENCY IS BUILT: EnergyPlus fan total efficiency = impeller x motor. Only impeller_efficiency is set here, because it is a property of the equipment class this scenario represents. The motor is looked up at runtime by the measure from the fan's brake horsepower via openstudio-standards fan_standard_minimum_motor_efficiency_and_size (ASHRAE 90.1-2019 enclosed 4-pole table: 0.855 at <=1 hp, 0.865 at 1-2, 0.895 at 2-5, 0.917 at 5-10, 0.924 at 10-15, 0.930 at 15-20 hp), so total efficiency varies with fan size and is not a single number. Static pressure is carried over from the fan being replaced and is never set here. Minimum flow fraction is stored here as minimum_flow_fraction; see minimum_flow_fraction_howto.",
                    "fan_efficiency_range_for_this_scenario": "RESULTING TOTAL EFFICIENCY for this scenario: 0.65 x motor = 0.556 (<=1 hp) to 0.605 (15-20 hp), the same as two_speed because the impeller is held at 0.65 for every scenario (see impeller_efficiency_notes). The variable-speed fan advantage comes only from the part-load curve and the lower turndown floor, not from efficiency. Previously this scenario received 0.6045 from a no-op call to fan_change_motor_efficiency, i.e. LOWER than the standard-efficiency two-speed unit's 0.630, which was backwards.",
                    "impeller_efficiency": 0.65,
                    "impeller_efficiency_notes": "openstudio-standards fan_baseline_impeller_efficiency: 0.65, the same wheel class as the constant-volume unit being replaced and as the two-speed scenarios. Deliberately NOT raised for the variable-speed units. A backward-curved or airfoil wheel on an ECM is plausibly better than a forward-curved wheel, but openstudio-standards does not distinguish fan types at all - Standards.Fan.rb returns 0.65 for every fan and carries an explicit '@todo Add fan type to data model' - and no source was found for a higher figure. An earlier draft used 0.70 here, which was worth about 4 points of annual fan savings on its own. Rather than rest that on an unsourced number, the impeller is held equal across baseline and all scenarios so the entire variable-speed advantage comes from the part-load curve and the turndown floor, both of which are documented. Decided 2026-08-27.",
